//! Read and write your own types in any data format, from the types alone.
//!
//! # When to use
//! Write `impl Schema for T {}` for a struct or a union, and read or write it with a format such
//! as `silk.schema.json.SchemaJson`. Declare what the type cannot say, such as a wire name or a
//! port range, in its [`Spec`]. Give a type its own `read`, `write` and `shape` when its wire form
//! differs from its fields, such as a port written as text, and build them from a codec chain with
//! [`Schemas.readVia`], [`Schemas.writeVia`] and [`Schemas.shapeVia`].
//!
//! # Details
//! A type is declared once, and the schema derives what to do from it. The default `read` and
//! `write` walk the type's public fields with `silk.core.reflect`, so a struct is read from an
//! object with one member per field, in any order, and written in declaration order. A union whose
//! variants have no fields is the name of its variant, such as `"Admin"`. A union with a variant
//! that has fields is an object whose `type` member names the variant and whose other members are
//! that variant's fields, such as `{"type": "Circle", "radius": 2}`; `Spec.tag` picks another
//! member name. An `Option` field may be absent, or `null`, when it is `None`, and is left out when
//! written.
//!
//! [`Spec`] holds what the types cannot say: a field whose name on the wire differs, the rules a
//! field's value follows (`Spec.between`, `Spec.length`, `Spec.items`, `Spec.pattern`), a default,
//! and whether unknown keys are rejected. The rules are data, so the same declaration checks a
//! read, words its errors, and fills the type's [`silk.schema.shape.Shapes`], which JSON Schema
//! and command line help are rendered from.
//!
//! A format implements [`SchemaReader`] and [`SchemaWriter`], so the same types read JSON today and
//! other formats later without changes.
//!
//! # Gotchas
//! Every field of a derived struct or variant must be public, because reflection sees only public
//! fields. A variant field cannot be named `type`, or whatever `Spec.tag` picks.
//!
//! # Examples
//! ## Read and write a struct as JSON
//! ```silk
//! import silk.core.effect { Effect }
//! import silk.core.result { Result }
//! import silk.mem.allocator { Allocator, OutOfMemoryError }
//! import silk.schema.issue { SchemaError }
//! import silk.schema.json { SchemaJson }
//! import silk.schema.schema { Schema, Spec }
//! import silk.text.string { String }
//!
//! struct User {
//!   pub id: u64,
//!   pub name: String,
//!   pub admin: bool,
//! }
//!
//! impl Schema for User {
//!   static fn spec() -> Spec<User> {
//!     return Spec<User>.of() |> Spec<User>.rename("id", "user_id") |> Spec<User>.length("name", 1, 64)
//!   }
//! }
//!
//! effect fn load() -> u64 ! SchemaError | OutOfMemoryError ? &mut Allocator {
//!   let user = run SchemaJson.decode<User>(b"{\"name\": \"Ada\", \"user_id\": 7, \"admin\": true}")
//!   if !user.admin { return 0 }
//!   return user.id
//! }
//!
//! pub effect fn main() -> i32 ! OutOfMemoryError {
//!   let mut allocator = Allocator.systemAllocatorProvider()
//!   let loaded = run Effect.result(load()) |> Effect.provideMut<Allocator>(&mut allocator)
//!   return match move loaded {
//!     Result<u64, SchemaError | OutOfMemoryError>.Success {value} => if value == 7 { 0 } else { 1 }
//!     Result<u64, SchemaError | OutOfMemoryError>.Failure {..} => 2
//!   }
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! @category Data formats

